From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Sean Dolbec <sdolbec1@gmail.com>
Date: Wed, 4 Nov 2026 14:56:03 -0500
Subject: [PATCH] drm/asahi: g16: Select the J773g DVFS table from its
 ADT

The G16 driver builds its firmware DVFS table from the sixteen
performance states of the original ADT, using a list of qualified
ten-row index layouts: J713's (four single states, then six
equal-voltage pairs) and J604's. The M4 Mac mini (J773g) has the J604
shape: five single states, five equal-voltage pairs and a single top
state at 1578 MHz. That is eleven distinct rows, and the J604 layout
drops the fifth low state to fit in ten.

Keep every state on J773g. Add an eleven-row J773g layout and give each
layout its row count. The board pins the row count it is qualified
for, ten on J713 and J604 and eleven on J773g, and only layouts with
that count are tried, so the J604 and J773g layouts of the same shape
cannot be confused. The table holds up to eleven rows with a count,
Main 0xfc0 carries that count instead of a constant 10, and the
encoder's ceiling is the last row.

Nothing changes on J713 or J604: their layouts and order are kept, the
host tests' measured J713 ADT gives the same ten rows, the J604-shaped
case still selects the J604 layout on a ten-row board, and the
qualified initdata goldens still match at ceilings 1, 4 and 9. The
host tests use synthetic voltage bins rather than a measured chip's,
and substitute them into those goldens before comparing; every other
byte is compared unchanged.

The runtime ceiling policy is unchanged: it publishes at most S9, and
the hardware performance state is still refused above 9. On J773g the
top row (S10, 1578 MHz) is in the tables but never requested; using it
needs evidence of its own. The reported maximum frequency is the top
row's.

Tested on a J773g, on an earlier integration/aurora (19f71a142419), with
a voltage-grouping selection that gives the same rows as the J773g
layout: the live ADT gave eleven rows, main/auxiliary 0/0, 338/338,
618/618, 796/796, 928/928, 1056/952, 1170/1053, 1278/1152, 1338/1204,
1470/1326 and 1578/1578 MHz. Voltage bins differ per chip; the table is
selected from its ADT. The firmware accepted these tables, reached
READY and completed a compute job. This layout-based version has not
been booted on hardware.

Signed-off-by: Sean Dolbec <sdolbec1@gmail.com>
[dylan: one tool trailer of the original message is not reproduced here]
